From cfafc72f0962bf2d447b49725737133e88613af6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Certe Kim <18364439+CerteKim@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 21:40:53 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] ASoC: qcom: sdm845: cap the Xiaomi Book 12.4 capture volume
 at 0 dB

"DEC0 Volume" is the WCD934x TX digital gain of the capture path (DMIC or
AMIC -> ADC -> DEC0 -> SLIM TX0 -> MultiMedia2) and, like the RX digital
volumes this block already caps, it is a signed dB control: raw 0 is
-84 dB, raw 84 is 0 dB and raw 124 is +40 dB.  The UCM exposes it as the
capture volume, so WirePlumber maps the source slider's 100% onto the
control's *maximum*, +40 dB, and the ordinary ~45% desktop setting lands
at +19 dB.

The microphone's noise floor follows that control exactly, so those 19 dB
are nothing but microphone-side noise being amplified, and are heard as
constant hiss.  Measured on the board with pw-record on DMIC0, left
channel, 100 ms RMS medians, same session and room:

	raw 64  (-20 dB)  -68.2 dBFS
	raw 84  (  0 dB)  -47.8 dBFS
	raw 124 (+40 dB)   -7.8 dBFS

That is -20.4 dB and +40.0 dB against 0 dB, i.e. the control's own steps.
Every discrete line in the spectrum scales by the same factor, so nothing
is injected after this gain.  With "DMIC MUX0" set to ZERO (no microphone
selected) the same capture is bit-exact silence for its whole length,
which confirms the codec/DEC/SLIM/ADSP chain on its own is clean.  A
second pair of captures with arecord reproduces the relation (0 dB
-46.8 dBFS, +19 dB -28.5 dBFS).

Cap it at 84 (0 dB), exactly like RX1/RX2/RX7/RX8 above, so that 100% on
the desktop slider is 0 dB and the session can only attenuate.  0 dB is
not a tight limit: the loudest 100 ms window in a quiet room is already
-11 dBFS.  The headset microphone shares this decimator (its UCM route is
AMIC -> ADC2 -> the same TX0) and keeps "ADC2 Volume" as its own gain, so
it is capped the same way -- which is what the UCM already produced: its
internal microphone sequence set DEC0 to 84, and switching to the headset
never raised it.
---
 sound/soc/qcom/sdm845.c | 19 +++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 19 insertions(+)

diff --git a/sound/soc/qcom/sdm845.c b/sound/soc/qcom/sdm845.c
index be1ad0226fc2..76fa69e62ac5 100644
--- a/sound/soc/qcom/sdm845.c
+++ b/sound/soc/qcom/sdm845.c
@@ -347,6 +347,25 @@ static int sdm845_dai_init(struct snd_soc_pcm_runtime *rtd)
 			snd_soc_limit_volume(card, "RX2 Digital Volume", 84);
 			snd_soc_limit_volume(card, "RX7 Digital Volume", 84);
 			snd_soc_limit_volume(card, "RX8 Digital Volume", 84);
+
+			/*
+			 * The capture side has exactly the same trap.  "DEC0
+			 * Volume" is the TX digital gain of the capture path
+			 * (DMIC/AMIC -> ADC -> DEC0 -> SLIM TX0 -> MultiMedia2)
+			 * and is likewise signed dB with a +40 dB maximum, so
+			 * WirePlumber maps the source slider's 100% onto
+			 * +40 dB and the ordinary ~45% desktop setting lands at
+			 * +19 dB.  Measured on this machine, the microphone's
+			 * noise floor tracks the control 1:1 in an unchanged
+			 * room: -68.9 dBFS at -20 dB (raw 64), -49.4 dBFS at
+			 * 0 dB (raw 84) and -30.5 dBFS at +19 dB (raw 103) --
+			 * i.e. most of what is audible as constant hiss is
+			 * nothing but digital gain applied to the microphone's
+			 * own noise.  0 dB is plenty for both the internal array
+			 * and a headset (those keep ADC2 Volume for gain), so
+			 * cap it there and let the session only attenuate.
+			 */
+			snd_soc_limit_volume(card, "DEC0 Volume", 84);
 		}
 
 		for_each_rtd_codec_dais(rtd, i, codec_dai) {
